## 2. Ambiguous-docker-match branch never sets `code` (duplicate `status` assignment)
**`server/src/service/infrastructure/networkService.js`**, ambiguous branch, hunk `@@ -243,23 +253,75 @@` (≈ lines 306-309):
```js
dockerResponse.status = 404;
dockerResponse.status = false;
dockerResponse.message = `Ambiguous container match ...`;
```
The second line overwrites the first, so `code` is never assigned — it stays `undefined` — while `status` ends as `false`. Compare the no-container branch directly above it, which correctly does `dockerResponse.code = 404; dockerResponse.status = false;`. Concrete failure: any caller that branches on the result integer code (e.g. `if (result.code === 404)`) receives `undefined` for the ambiguous case, so it can't distinguish "ambiguous" from other failures; the ambiguous path also reports a nonsensical `status: 404`. This is a copy-paste defect.

## 3. Unknown `matchMethod` now unconditionally fails a monitor; plus unhandled `TypeError`/`SyntaxError`
**`server/src/service/infrastructure/networkService.js`**, `requestHttp`, hunk `@@ -147,16 +149,24 @@` (≈ lines 154-176).

New logic:
```js
let ok = false;
if (matchMethod === "equal") ok = payload === expectedValue;
if (matchMethod === "include") ok = payload.includes(expectedValue);
if (matchMethod === "regex") ok = new RegExp(expectedValue).test(payload);
if (ok === true) { return httpResponse; }
else { httpResponse.code = 500; httpResponse.status = false; return httpResponse; }
```
Defects:
- If `matchMethod` is any value other than `equal`/`include`/`regex` (e.g., a misspelling, or a previously-supported method), `ok` stays `false` and the monitor is marked **DOWN (code 500)** even when the value matches. The old code did not fail on unknown match methods — it just logged.
- `payload.includes(expectedValue)` throws `TypeError` when `payload` is a JSON object (`.includes` is not a function); `new RegExp(expectedValue)` throws `SyntaxError` if the configured value is not a valid regex (e.g. `(`). These are now reached on every check and are not caught here, so a malformed expectation crashes the check.
